  <blockquote class="evidence">Meta's own ads guide sets a Reels video ad at 9:16 and 1440 by 2560 pixels, up to 4GB and up to 15 minutes on Instagram, and tells advertisers to consider leaving at least 14 percent of the top, 35 percent of the bottom and 6 percent of each side free of text, logos and other key elements.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: Meta's Facebook Ads Guide, Awareness video ad specs for Instagram Reels and Facebook Reels, read 10 and 15 September 2026, a primary reading scoped to those placements and that objective. https://www.facebook.com/business/ads-guide/update/video/instagram-reels</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">TikTok's ads help center recommends a vertical 9:16 in-feed video at 540 by 960 pixels or more, accepts 16:9 and 1:1, and caps the file at 500 MB. Auction in-feed ads run up to 10 minutes at 516 kbps or more. Reservation in-feed and TikTok TopView run 5 to 60 seconds, recommend 9 to 15, need at least 2,500 kbps, and must carry sound.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: TikTok ads help center, auction in-feed specifications updated June 2026, reservation in-feed specifications updated July 2025 and TopView specifications updated June 2026, read 10 and 15 September 2026, primary readings each scoped to its buying type. https://ads.tiktok.com/help/article/tiktok-auction-in-feed-ads</p>

  <p>The China side comes with a count instead of a page.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">The most commonly published Douyin post format is a 9:16 vertical frame at 1080x1920 pixels. Four of four independent sources that name a native ratio give 9:16, and all four give 1080x1920.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: 12 independent published references, 11 Chinese-language and 1 English-language, collected 10 September 2026, counted per domain and reported as the most commonly published figure. Modal value, not a primary reading.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">The most commonly published RedNote cover format is a 3:4 vertical frame at 1080x1440 pixels. Fourteen of fourteen independent sources that name a default name 3:4, and thirteen give 1080x1440 as its pixel size, with a higher-density 1242-pixel-wide variant appearing on three.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: 20 independent published references in Chinese and English collected 10 September 2026, counted per domain and reported as the most commonly published figure, not as a value read from the platform.</p>

  <p>Two kinds of evidence sit in one table, and the stronger half mustn't lend its confidence to the weaker one. A Meta cell can be checked against a page this afternoon. A Douyin cell, only against your own account.</p>

  <h2>Why aren't Douyin and TikTok one ad spec?</h2>

  <p>Because the apps publish differently, and where both publish the same product the numbers differ. Both carry a TopView. They do not carry the same TopView.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Douyin TopView runs a video of 10 to 60 seconds. The first 3 seconds play full screen with nothing on top, a conversion component fades in after that, and from the fourth second the ad sits as the first post in the feed. Four of five Douyin-specific sources give the 10 to 60 second window. The fifth gives 5 to 60, which is TikTok's published range.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: 5 Douyin-specific published sources, Chinese-language, pages dated 2019 to 2021 and 2025, collected 10 September 2026. Modal value, not a primary reading.</p>

  <p>That fifth source is the pattern in miniature. TikTok's figures are easy to find, so they get copied onto Douyin pages, and a buyer searching in English can't tell which app a number belongs to. The white rule shows the same drift in the other direction.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">TikTok bars plain white in the first 3 seconds of a TopView video, because its logo is hard to see against it. That rule belongs to TikTok. No Douyin source in the collection used for this page publishes it.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: TikTok ads help center, TopView ad specifications, updated June 2026, read 10 and 15 September 2026, checked against a 17-source Douyin paid-ad collection of 10 September 2026. Primary reading of the TikTok page; the Douyin side is an absence in a counted collection.</p>

  <p>Then there is the safe zone, which TikTok does not publish as a number at all.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">TikTok does not print a safe-zone inset on its in-feed or TopView spec pages. It says the safe zone shrinks as the caption gets longer and changes with orientation and add-ons, and it supplies template files instead. The pixel insets that circulate for TikTok come from other publishers, and five sets checked for this page did not match one another.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: three TikTok ads help center specification pages read in full on 10 and 15 September 2026, plus five third-party TikTok inset sets traced to their pages. Primary reading for what TikTok publishes, and an observation about the figures others publish.</p>

  <p>So a producer arrives at Douyin with a TikTok template that TikTok says moves with the caption (the downloaded file is the only way to check that half), and a Douyin figure that no two sources agree on. The <a href="/resources/insights/douyin-video-specs-safe-zones">Douyin video specs and safe zones article</a> carries the full spread. The <a href="/resources/insights/douyin-ad-creative-specs-by-format">Douyin ad creative specs article</a> carries the paid formats by age of source.</p>

  <h2>Where does a Meta-safe layout leak on Douyin and RedNote?</h2>

  <p>On the sides and at the top, by a few dozen pixels. Not at the bottom.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Only two Chinese-language sources publish Douyin-specific safe-zone insets, and they do not agree. The top band appears as 220 pixels and as 15 percent of frame height, about 288 pixels, on two pages of the same site. The bottom band appears as 200, 288 and 380 pixels. No Chinese source publishes a width for the right-side button column at all.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: 5 independent published references addressing a Douyin safe zone, Chinese-language, collected 10 September 2026, every inset converted to a 1080x1920 canvas before comparison. Range published, contested, not read from the platform.</p>

  <p>Take the largest published figure on each side and Meta's percentages on the same canvas, and the two boxes almost coincide.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Text placed to Meta's Reels safe zone on a 1080 by 1920 frame can sit as close as 65 pixels to each side and 269 pixels to the top. The published Douyin figures, taken at their largest, keep critical content 108 pixels from each side and 288 from the top. That leaves a strip of about 43 pixels on each side and 19 at the top where a Meta-safe line falls outside the Douyin box. Meta's 35 percent bottom reservation clears every published Douyin bottom band.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: arithmetic on Meta's Reels safe-zone percentages, read on its ads guide 10 and 15 September 2026, and the derived Douyin safe box from five Douyin sources collected 10 September 2026. Derived, not a measurement of either app.</p>

  <p>Meta's own help article on the subject, which served no text on 10 September and a full page on 15 September, adds one figure the ads guide does not.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Meta's help article on safe zones states the rule in words: for 9:16 ads in Stories, Reels and Feed, keep the top, bottom and sides free of key creative elements, text and logos, and on screens taller than 9:16 the creative may be zoomed, cropping whatever sits outside. It adds one figure the ads guide pages do not: a Reels ad that carries a disclaimer should leave the bottom 40 percent free.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: Meta Business Help Center, About text overlays and the safe zone for ads in Stories and Reels, read 15 September 2026, a primary reading of that article only; the 14, 35 and 6 percent figures come from the ads guide pages. https://www.facebook.com/business/help/980593475366490</p>

  <p>RedNote changes the shape of the question rather than the margin. Its feed shows a 3:4 cover first, and the published accounts of how that cover is cropped disagree with each other, so the region that survives all of them is the center square. One box clears everything published for the four apps.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">On a 1080 by 1920 vertical master, keeping text, faces and product between 108 and 972 pixels across and between 420 and 1248 pixels down clears Meta's Reels safe zone, every Douyin inset in circulation, and the center of a 3:4 RedNote cover above its deepest published bottom band. TikTok adds no number, because TikTok publishes none: check the file against its template for the caption you plan to run.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: derived 15 September 2026 from Meta's Reels safe-zone percentages (ads guide, primary), a Douyin safe box from five sources, and a RedNote center square and 288-pixel bottom reservation from ten and eight sources, assuming centered cuts. A composition rule derived from published figures, not a platform rule.</p>

  <p>That box is 864 by 828 pixels, smaller than any one platform asks for on its own. Shooting once costs that margin.</p>

  <h2>Which parts of a Western cut survive the move?</h2>

  <p>The input is a vertical ad built for Reels or TikTok in-feed: 9:16, 15 to 30 seconds, sound on, text placed to Meta's Reels safe zone, exported at Meta's recommended 1440 by 2560 in MP4, with no separate cover. Every verdict below is derived from the rows above. None is a platform rule, and centered cuts are an assumption no source states.</p>

